WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.868
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.327 --> 00:00:08.410
GABRIELE GARZOGLIO: My
name is Gabriele Garzoglio.

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:11.260
I am a cloud consultant with
the Professional Services

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:14.100
Organization at Google.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:17.650
I'm here with my collaborators
from Cardinal Health,

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:21.640
Ed Hammond, Louis Verzi,
and Walker Miller.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:25.740
The way we have structured our
talk today is in four sections.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:29.140
We'll start talking about the
challenges and best practices

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:30.970
about application migrations.

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:33.850
I'll tell you how PSO,
the Professional Services

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:37.870
Organization, can help you with
application migration using

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:40.860
a methodology called
Cloud Sprints.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.580
I'll let you hear
directly from Cardinal

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:45.670
Health about their experience
with Cloud Sprint or the use

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:46.649
case.

00:00:46.649 --> 00:00:48.940
And then I'll come back and
talk a bit more technically

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:52.340
about what we do with you
when we do a Cloud Sprint.

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.810
When we start working
with a customer

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:00.040
to talk about
application migrations,

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:02.820
the first thing we do
is assess the maturity

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:04.200
of their infrastructure.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.030
And there are many
ways to do this,

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:09.210
but one way is
really understanding

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:12.040
how much cloud ready they are.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.080
And so, if you
look on the y-axis,

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:17.010
there is the on-prem
amount of computation

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:19.680
that the customer does
versus how ready they

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.540
are for public cloud.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:25.740
On the x-axis, you can actually
see whether their operations

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:28.710
are more classical,
sys-admin-oriented type

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:33.810
of operations versus more
cloud native CI/CD-based DevOps

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:35.100
operations.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.550
And of course,
most customers want

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:41.880
to be on the top right
corner, where, of course,

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.660
you have the productivity.

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:46.800
You have the
portability of the code.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.270
You know, you pay
for what you do.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:52.260
But what we often observe is
that customers are actually

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.090
on the lower right--

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:56.400
actually, lower left,
where everything

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.100
is more rigid and expensive.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.140
And so, how can we help--

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:06.430
we, PSO-- help you move towards
the top right corner here.

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:09.870
And of course, we work with you
to understand how to integrate

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:14.460
your CI/CD processes with GCP.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:18.540
And the other thing we do is
also to help you with lifting,

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:22.350
shifting your virtual
machines or your servers

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:27.340
from your on-prem
environment into GCP.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:32.100
So as we do this, we
observe that there

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:35.650
are challenges that everybody
faces during these migrations.

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:38.490
And there are both business
challenges as well as

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:40.317
technical challenges.

00:02:40.317 --> 00:02:42.150
I mean, some of these
things-- without going

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:43.358
through the full list, right?

00:02:43.358 --> 00:02:46.130
But often, we put
together teams,

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:47.780
and then we see
that there aren't

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:53.130
enough experts in a
given area to actually do

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:55.710
things hands-on on the cloud.

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:57.630
Often there are
decisions that needs

00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:01.270
to be taken about the
direction of the architecture.

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:03.450
And you know, there are
maybe no decision makers

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:06.720
available at that
time to talk to.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.460
Some concerns about the
flexibility of the design.

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:13.560
Sometimes, you know,
there are other concerns.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:16.200
The way we like to
organize these activities

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.870
is really as a
agile methodologies,

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:23.520
where we do iterations of the
designs and implementations

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:27.690
until we get to a point
where you want to be.

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:30.390
Some of the technical
challenges is really

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:32.670
about the lack of
automation culture.

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:35.820
I talked about helping you
integrate CI/CD processes

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:38.490
with our cloud.

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:43.590
The closer you are to the
right-hand of the graph,

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:45.750
the better it is.

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:49.830
Sometimes, there is
the idea that we want

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:51.180
to do everything all at once.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:53.460
And, you know, the
boil the ocean type

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:56.650
of activity, which goes
together with the expectations

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:58.500
mismatch on the left.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:02.080
And so, as a cloud consultant,
my job is often say,

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:03.350
let's go over this carefully.

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:08.610
Make sure that we identify some
good first mover so that we

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:10.680
can create an
infrastructure for you

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.900
and then migrate into
this infrastructure

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:17.040
a good application, where
you can start testing things

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:20.430
and really popularize the work
that you are doing with us.

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:27.750
We structure the
journey of a customer

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:34.130
through our service offerings
in these four different type

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:35.220
of activities.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:36.290
We do assessment.

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:38.190
We plan with you.

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:41.760
We talk about how
you can deploy and do

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:44.350
the migration of your
applications on the cloud.

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:47.220
And once you are
there, we can consults

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:51.000
on how you can
take best advantage

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.140
of the cloud with
some optimizations.

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:55.320
So after you have
done a lift and shift,

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:57.540
you can start using your
environment already.

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:02.500
And then you can start
using native cloud services.

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:06.870
And, you know, this is a whole
journey that, if you want,

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:08.790
PSO can help you with.

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:11.910
And for this talk today,
I'm going to talk more

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:13.960
about the foundation.

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:17.430
So how we design foundations
for your environment in UCP

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:19.560
to receive your
applications, and then

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:23.820
talk a bit about how to
do the implementation.

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:28.070
And throughout this
journey, we keep in mind

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:34.500
a few best practices that
I can tell you about.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:38.130
The sooner you have a
production-quality foundational

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:40.880
environment, the sooner
you can start actually

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:43.490
using your cloud for real.

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:46.750
There is a lot of focus on
our side to really automate

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:49.190
the deployment of
foundational elements

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:53.070
and really designing them
for your actual needs.

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:56.390
In everything that we do, we
always consider security first

00:05:56.390 --> 00:05:58.580
From The first
discussion we have

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:01.850
until we get to the
optimization phase,

00:06:01.850 --> 00:06:03.830
and really try to
understand not only

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:07.700
how to deploy the foundation
in an automated fashion,

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:10.610
but also how you can
automate the deployment

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:13.160
of your applications.

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:16.970
As I mentioned before, we
organize these activities

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:23.990
in Springs, where we can
actually do iterative design

00:06:23.990 --> 00:06:26.750
and implementation,
so that every time you

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:29.630
can get a feeling for
how the infrastructure is

00:06:29.630 --> 00:06:31.370
going to work.

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:34.100
And then I talked about,
again, making sure

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:38.240
that a first mover application,
the one that you start

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:41.060
moving into your application
to popularize the work you're

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:43.690
doing, is a good one.

00:06:43.690 --> 00:06:45.890
And so, I'll focus
a bit in this talk

00:06:45.890 --> 00:06:50.749
about the foundational
aspect of things.

00:06:50.749 --> 00:06:52.540
And when I talk about
foundational aspects,

00:06:52.540 --> 00:06:54.990
these are the things
that I'm talking about.

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:57.070
Again, selection of
first mover, which

00:06:57.070 --> 00:06:59.200
is the important piece here.

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:02.530
And then, we want
to do user identity,

00:07:02.530 --> 00:07:05.670
either through G Suite
or cloud identity.

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:08.830
Resource organization and access
management go hand-in-hand.

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:10.120
I'll talk more about this.

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:15.100
How you decide to
structure your organization

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:19.540
has strong implication on how
you want to implement policies

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:24.160
on this hierarchy of resources.

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:28.270
We always recommend using groups
and roles to access resources.

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:30.610
I'll talk more about
that at the end.

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:32.830
And then, of course,
we spend a lot of time

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:33.880
doing network design.

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:36.040
So talking about the
connectivity to on-prem

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:39.400
or to another cloud, the
topology of the network,

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:41.820
and all the network
services, DNS,

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:44.530
and so on, so forth,
BGP, et cetera.

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:45.280
Security.

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:48.430
Again, we can work with you
to understand your security

00:07:48.430 --> 00:07:50.860
objectives, and
then understand how

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:53.800
to implement that proactive
security controls,

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:56.650
and then what we call reactive
security controls in the sense

00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:59.110
that we can have
scanners that check

00:07:59.110 --> 00:08:02.530
your environment
periodically and can fire up

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:07.360
alarms when the configuration
is not what you expected.

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:09.820
Talking about the type
of computing environment

00:08:09.820 --> 00:08:11.940
that are important for
you, and then, of course,

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:14.780
start talking about operations,
because at the end of the day,

00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:16.910
you do your migration,
but guess what?

00:08:16.910 --> 00:08:19.450
You're going to have to change
somewhat your operations

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:23.860
to make sure that you take
best advantage of the cloud.

00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:25.900
Now, how do we do it in PSO?

00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:28.930
How can we help you with this?

00:08:31.480 --> 00:08:37.960
For application migration, the
typical approach that we take

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:40.870
is offering you a
consulting service

00:08:40.870 --> 00:08:42.549
called the Cloud Sprint.

00:08:42.549 --> 00:08:46.030
And the Cloud Sprint
is really one week

00:08:46.030 --> 00:08:50.110
of intense work preceded
by some preparation, where

00:08:50.110 --> 00:08:51.070
we work together.

00:08:51.070 --> 00:08:54.790
We do together a
workshop for a full week.

00:08:54.790 --> 00:08:57.510
And we work
elbow-to-elbow to design

00:08:57.510 --> 00:09:01.540
a foundational
infrastructure and start

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:04.600
working towards implementing
this infrastructure with you,

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:07.030
and then migrate maybe a
couple of applications, one

00:09:07.030 --> 00:09:08.830
or two applications.

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:12.880
We do this, essentially, with
identifying their workloads,

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:16.480
the good first mover, building
the foundational elements,

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:19.450
migrating the workload,
and then understanding

00:09:19.450 --> 00:09:22.720
at the end of this
week, once we are off,

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:25.090
how can we keep helping
you, or how can you

00:09:25.090 --> 00:09:27.625
take this and move it
forward by yourself.

00:09:31.030 --> 00:09:33.040
We talk about GCP.

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:37.090
And it's a chance
for us to educate you

00:09:37.090 --> 00:09:39.370
and for us to learn
from you on what you

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:42.670
are doing your infrastructure.

00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:44.170
Security, as I
said, is always very

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:46.810
important in everything we do.

00:09:46.810 --> 00:09:48.970
Select the first
mover, and then the

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:51.160
really instantiate the
production-ready cloud

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:54.610
foundation, and migrate
the production workload.

00:09:54.610 --> 00:09:56.530
When I say
production-ready, I'm saying

00:09:56.530 --> 00:09:59.170
that all the elements of a
production infrastructure

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:02.080
is going to be available to you.

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:04.960
It may require still
some work to tailor it

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:06.680
to your specific needs.

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:09.010
So, for example,
when you do auditing,

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:14.800
we can integrate the GCP
logs with your same solution,

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:18.250
whether you use Splunk
or some other solution.

00:10:18.250 --> 00:10:23.200
But of course, the alarms
that you want to trigger on

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:25.990
are going to often be
application-specific.

00:10:25.990 --> 00:10:29.240
So then, when you get to
the point of configuring

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:32.000
the new infrastructure
to respond to your needs,

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:34.840
then you can move it from
production-ready to actually

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:35.758
production.

00:10:38.830 --> 00:10:42.300
This is a typical way
we organize the sprints.

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:45.600
They are meant to be
very parallel in nature.

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:50.880
And we start generally with
this technical infrastructure

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:52.750
kickoff, where we
talk about GCP,

00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:55.380
and we learn from you
about your infrastructure.

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:58.740
We spend most of
the day doing design

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:02.400
on the first day, elbow-to-elbow
whiteboarding sessions.

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:04.140
And then already
the first day, we

00:11:04.140 --> 00:11:06.480
sit together on your computers.

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:10.020
We look at what we have
decided to tackle first

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:12.690
of the foundational
elements, and then

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:15.210
implement it in GCP with you.

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:17.370
Always talk about
security and then

00:11:17.370 --> 00:11:22.200
split into multiple
work streams, where

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:25.950
each work streams tackle one
of the foundational elements

00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:28.710
plus the application
development, so

00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:31.230
the application deployment,
so the actual migration

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:32.910
of the workload.

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:36.990
If you look at the
yellow lines, you

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:39.600
will recognize the
foundational elements

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:43.040
that I was discussing before.

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:45.200
One thing that is important
to work well on these

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:48.200
together is that there is a
need to have enough people that

00:11:48.200 --> 00:11:51.680
are experts in these various
areas in your business

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:53.560
so that we can actually split.

00:11:53.560 --> 00:11:55.520
We'll bring enough
people, enough engineer,

00:11:55.520 --> 00:11:59.060
enough consultants, but we
need to be able to pair up.

00:11:59.060 --> 00:12:02.090
The last day is
making an assessment

00:12:02.090 --> 00:12:06.620
of how well we did during
the workshop, and, in fact,

00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:11.450
how ready the infrastructure
is for production activities.

00:12:11.450 --> 00:12:16.310
Every day, we take that ideal
situation and we reassess

00:12:16.310 --> 00:12:17.520
it based on your needs.

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:21.350
This is a real-life example
of something that we did.

00:12:21.350 --> 00:12:25.070
And we agree on how to split in
groups to actually accomplish

00:12:25.070 --> 00:12:27.440
at the end of the
day what we want

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:30.560
to do for your specific needs.

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:32.900
And this is an example
of the assessment

00:12:32.900 --> 00:12:34.370
that I was discussing before.

00:12:34.370 --> 00:12:37.040
You see the two columns,
the workshop outcome,

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:39.440
and the production
readiness column,

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:40.940
how well we did
during the workshop,

00:12:40.940 --> 00:12:45.290
and how ready the infrastructure
is by topic, which is typically

00:12:45.290 --> 00:12:48.390
the foundational elements that
we have identified for your use

00:12:48.390 --> 00:12:48.890
case.

00:12:52.370 --> 00:12:55.400
One of the best
ways we can be sure

00:12:55.400 --> 00:12:58.760
that we know where the
percentage of errors

00:12:58.760 --> 00:13:03.650
in deploying the infrastructure
is by automation.

00:13:03.650 --> 00:13:07.880
We have libraries of
infrastructure as code

00:13:07.880 --> 00:13:09.710
that we share with you.

00:13:09.710 --> 00:13:11.660
And we work with
you elbow-to-elbow

00:13:11.660 --> 00:13:15.380
to configure so that
we can collaborate

00:13:15.380 --> 00:13:17.180
on the infrastructure
and the code

00:13:17.180 --> 00:13:20.150
of your foundational
infrastructure.

00:13:20.150 --> 00:13:21.950
Of course, the
consistency is improved,

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:26.360
because you can check
the stuff into git,

00:13:26.360 --> 00:13:31.630
and then avoid the manual
error by doing peer reviews,

00:13:31.630 --> 00:13:35.600
typical debugging techniques
that you can do now on code.

00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:38.990
And of course-- and force
policies proactively.

00:13:38.990 --> 00:13:42.680
After this talk,
around 4 o'clock,

00:13:42.680 --> 00:13:45.080
my colleague is going to
talk about more details

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:48.440
of this infrastructure
that we have,

00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:50.270
the cloud foundation
and the automation.

00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:56.260
I forget exactly where.

00:13:56.260 --> 00:13:57.170
The hotel?

00:13:57.170 --> 00:13:59.090
The national hotel?

00:13:59.090 --> 00:14:00.380
It's not in this building.

00:14:00.380 --> 00:14:03.170
It's one of the
building that's outside.

00:14:03.170 --> 00:14:04.700
What I can show
you is an example

00:14:04.700 --> 00:14:08.230
of what we do, for example,
for provisioning projects.

00:14:08.230 --> 00:14:10.760
I mean, creating
a project is not

00:14:10.760 --> 00:14:13.610
everything you need to start.

00:14:13.610 --> 00:14:18.470
You need to then instantiate
the computing environment

00:14:18.470 --> 00:14:21.270
that you want to use,
enable building, enable

00:14:21.270 --> 00:14:24.550
the APIs that you care,
create the service accounts,

00:14:24.550 --> 00:14:27.210
IAM policies, and
so on, so forth.

00:14:27.210 --> 00:14:32.270
And you can script all of
this by using our templates.

00:14:32.270 --> 00:14:38.660
Now, I want to give Ed a chance
to talk about their experience

00:14:38.660 --> 00:14:39.620
with GCP.

00:14:46.110 --> 00:14:47.070
ED HAMMOND: Is this on?

00:14:47.070 --> 00:14:49.140
Yeah, OK.

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:52.650
My name's Ed Hammond, and
I'm with Cardinal Health.

00:14:52.650 --> 00:14:57.240
Cardinal Health is based in
Columbus, Ohio, Dublin, Ohio,

00:14:57.240 --> 00:14:59.100
suburb of Columbus.

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:01.350
And our mission is to
make health care more

00:15:01.350 --> 00:15:05.640
efficient for providers
and, in the end,

00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:08.470
trying to help our patients
have better health care.

00:15:11.530 --> 00:15:15.210
So our cloud philosophy
has been very simple.

00:15:15.210 --> 00:15:18.680
We want to enable agile
teams, development teams

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:20.840
to be very effective.

00:15:20.840 --> 00:15:23.030
Speed, agility, cost
efficiency, those

00:15:23.030 --> 00:15:25.010
are all the great buzzwords.

00:15:25.010 --> 00:15:28.880
Traditionally, Cardinal Health
has been a shared services--

00:15:28.880 --> 00:15:31.670
classic shared
services model, where

00:15:31.670 --> 00:15:35.390
there are silos of specialists
doing specific tasks.

00:15:35.390 --> 00:15:37.940
And we're trying to
break down those walls

00:15:37.940 --> 00:15:42.830
and enable agile teams
to be self-sufficient,

00:15:42.830 --> 00:15:47.660
so they're able to quickly
adapt and experiment

00:15:47.660 --> 00:15:49.610
with new technologies.

00:15:49.610 --> 00:15:53.900
The GCP project design, the
notion of a GCP project,

00:15:53.900 --> 00:15:58.430
fits our model perfectly for
doing that because that helps

00:15:58.430 --> 00:16:00.740
us set the security
policies and everything

00:16:00.740 --> 00:16:04.460
for those agile teams.

00:16:04.460 --> 00:16:06.050
The security by design.

00:16:06.050 --> 00:16:09.920
We started with building in
the controls right up front.

00:16:09.920 --> 00:16:13.460
What you'll notice right
in GCP is that the project

00:16:13.460 --> 00:16:15.450
is a unit of authorization.

00:16:15.450 --> 00:16:18.470
So you can set authorization
controls at the project,

00:16:18.470 --> 00:16:21.740
so we have tight controls
about how we create projects.

00:16:21.740 --> 00:16:26.210
But beyond that, we delegate the
controls to the app-dev teams.

00:16:26.210 --> 00:16:29.180
And the self-service and
automation as Gabriele

00:16:29.180 --> 00:16:30.020
mentioned.

00:16:30.020 --> 00:16:32.750
You want to always provide
tools, documentation,

00:16:32.750 --> 00:16:34.550
and consulting services.

00:16:34.550 --> 00:16:37.520
Rather than having a large
shared services team,

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:39.650
we want to have a
small cloud expert

00:16:39.650 --> 00:16:44.210
team that can go around and
help DevOps teams be sufficient,

00:16:44.210 --> 00:16:46.700
self-sufficient in the cloud.

00:16:46.700 --> 00:16:48.200
So we provide documentation.

00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:51.230
We have a wiki that has
lots of documentation on it.

00:16:51.230 --> 00:16:53.460
We consult and help
people build things.

00:16:53.460 --> 00:16:57.000
We have a number of colleagues
in the audience that do that.

00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:59.030
But at the end of the
day, we want our teams

00:16:59.030 --> 00:17:01.850
to be working on their own,
so that they can really

00:17:01.850 --> 00:17:06.290
experiment, test,
learn, and grow.

00:17:06.290 --> 00:17:07.849
So how do we do this?

00:17:07.849 --> 00:17:10.550
One of the key things
we learned early on

00:17:10.550 --> 00:17:13.849
was that the network
is pretty key.

00:17:13.849 --> 00:17:18.440
So our design is basically
to use two host projects--

00:17:18.440 --> 00:17:21.380
if you're familiar with shared
VPCs and host projects--

00:17:21.380 --> 00:17:23.510
to use two host projects,
one for production,

00:17:23.510 --> 00:17:27.650
one for non-production, and
create shared VPCs under that.

00:17:27.650 --> 00:17:31.730
Then the project teams
are using service projects

00:17:31.730 --> 00:17:35.330
that consumes those PCs.

00:17:35.330 --> 00:17:38.900
We have tight controls over
internet Ingress and Egress

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:39.980
patterns.

00:17:39.980 --> 00:17:42.140
We're using various
different security appliances

00:17:42.140 --> 00:17:45.680
and techniques for doing
those kinds of things.

00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:49.610
But we worked very hard at
setting that up upfront.

00:17:49.610 --> 00:17:53.510
So before the Cloud Sprint
engagement with PSO,

00:17:53.510 --> 00:17:58.402
who did a great job, by the
way, we had all this set up.

00:17:58.402 --> 00:18:00.860
It took us about three months
working with our account team

00:18:00.860 --> 00:18:02.750
to learn how it all
worked, because we

00:18:02.750 --> 00:18:04.820
went from zero knowledge
to three months,

00:18:04.820 --> 00:18:06.440
we had it all built
and ready to go,

00:18:06.440 --> 00:18:09.530
and we started going
into the sprint.

00:18:09.530 --> 00:18:12.050
Our application teams
manage their own projects.

00:18:12.050 --> 00:18:14.450
Again, typically, they'll
have one service project

00:18:14.450 --> 00:18:17.600
for production, one service
project for non-production.

00:18:17.600 --> 00:18:22.130
And then we set up the IAM
roles, customize IAM roles,

00:18:22.130 --> 00:18:24.170
at the organizational level.

00:18:24.170 --> 00:18:27.391
And we delegate that down and
use service accounts and AD

00:18:27.391 --> 00:18:27.890
groups.

00:18:27.890 --> 00:18:31.610
We map them over for owner
and editor privileges

00:18:31.610 --> 00:18:34.400
within each one of the projects.

00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:36.920
The service accounts
for VPC firewall rules,

00:18:36.920 --> 00:18:38.750
that's really key to us.

00:18:38.750 --> 00:18:41.750
So that when you
create an instance,

00:18:41.750 --> 00:18:43.880
it's using a service
account, and that

00:18:43.880 --> 00:18:46.310
gives the authorization
for that instance.

00:18:46.310 --> 00:18:48.660
We're also doing it
for routing rules,

00:18:48.660 --> 00:18:50.900
so it can do the
Ingress/Egress patterns,

00:18:50.900 --> 00:18:55.010
as well as for the VPC
firewall rules for east, west,

00:18:55.010 --> 00:18:58.070
and north, south traffic.

00:18:58.070 --> 00:19:00.980
As Gabriele mentioned,
we always want

00:19:00.980 --> 00:19:02.270
to have everything scripted.

00:19:02.270 --> 00:19:05.990
So wherever something is
well-known, well understood,

00:19:05.990 --> 00:19:09.260
and repeatable, we try
and do it in scripts.

00:19:09.260 --> 00:19:12.580
So when we built the networks,
are project creation process

00:19:12.580 --> 00:19:13.820
is all scripted.

00:19:13.820 --> 00:19:16.040
Creating service accounts
and so forth, those

00:19:16.040 --> 00:19:18.590
are all scripts that we use.

00:19:18.590 --> 00:19:20.090
With that, I'm going
to turn it over

00:19:20.090 --> 00:19:24.370
to my colleague, Louis Verzi,
who is a cloud evangelist.

00:19:30.330 --> 00:19:32.760
LOUIS VERZI: So they let us
make up our titles when we

00:19:32.760 --> 00:19:33.990
were doing this presentation.

00:19:33.990 --> 00:19:37.980
So I thought about what
I do on a daily basis.

00:19:37.980 --> 00:19:40.900
And my official title
is senior engineer,

00:19:40.900 --> 00:19:43.830
but I feel like my
job at Cardinal Health

00:19:43.830 --> 00:19:47.010
is really making sure
everyone understands

00:19:47.010 --> 00:19:48.866
what the benefits
of the cloud are,

00:19:48.866 --> 00:19:50.490
and what they can
achieve in the cloud.

00:19:50.490 --> 00:19:54.792
So that's where I came up with
the cloud evangelist title.

00:19:54.792 --> 00:19:57.250
I want to tell you a little
bit about our sprint engagement

00:19:57.250 --> 00:20:00.990
and how that
experience was for us.

00:20:00.990 --> 00:20:02.670
And there's really
two messages that I

00:20:02.670 --> 00:20:07.510
want to share with you that
I want you to take from this.

00:20:07.510 --> 00:20:10.920
The first is to be ready
or be prepared going

00:20:10.920 --> 00:20:12.710
into your sprint engagement.

00:20:12.710 --> 00:20:15.870
And the second is to
view the sprint, really,

00:20:15.870 --> 00:20:20.700
as taking you the last mile
into your journey in the cloud.

00:20:20.700 --> 00:20:24.690
So obviously, Google's
going to meet you wherever

00:20:24.690 --> 00:20:26.490
you are in the process.

00:20:26.490 --> 00:20:30.000
So if you're on the
first mile, don't feel

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:32.680
like it's not time to engage.

00:20:32.680 --> 00:20:35.820
But for us, really, taking
us the last mile really

00:20:35.820 --> 00:20:37.860
helped out a lot.

00:20:37.860 --> 00:20:43.380
So plan ahead, understand
the technology,

00:20:43.380 --> 00:20:45.930
play around with the services.

00:20:45.930 --> 00:20:48.870
We had four to
five engineers that

00:20:48.870 --> 00:20:51.780
were researching Google
technologies in the two

00:20:51.780 --> 00:20:54.690
months leading up
to our sprint, kind

00:20:54.690 --> 00:20:59.820
of talking about
our culture, how

00:20:59.820 --> 00:21:02.790
we wanted to Google
technologies to fit in,

00:21:02.790 --> 00:21:07.090
really, how we wanted
to design overall.

00:21:07.090 --> 00:21:14.100
And so, by the time the
PSO engineers came on-site,

00:21:14.100 --> 00:21:16.320
we had a lot of our
design complete.

00:21:16.320 --> 00:21:20.340
That allowed us to vet
a lot of our designs

00:21:20.340 --> 00:21:22.170
and make sure that
we were thinking

00:21:22.170 --> 00:21:23.790
about all of the things that
we should have been thinking

00:21:23.790 --> 00:21:25.740
about, that we weren't
leaving anything out,

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:29.440
that we weren't being
stupid about some stuff.

00:21:29.440 --> 00:21:33.790
And so it was really
helpful for us.

00:21:33.790 --> 00:21:36.690
So I'd encourage you,
if you're starting,

00:21:36.690 --> 00:21:38.670
if you're early
on in the process,

00:21:38.670 --> 00:21:42.420
to do some research on
VPC strategies, cloud

00:21:42.420 --> 00:21:48.240
identity, resource
hierarchy, and custom roles.

00:21:48.240 --> 00:21:54.000
Those were some key
areas that we understood

00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:57.780
where we wanted to be when
the PSO folks showed up,

00:21:57.780 --> 00:22:03.330
so that we could make the
most progress as possible.

00:22:03.330 --> 00:22:05.460
So being prepared
allowed us to get

00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:09.840
into details like how
to deploy and configure

00:22:09.840 --> 00:22:18.270
for SETI, building a billion
showback model and data studio,

00:22:18.270 --> 00:22:21.090
defining our
organizational policies.

00:22:21.090 --> 00:22:24.380
So it was still in
alpha when we deployed,

00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:27.270
but the organizational
policies really

00:22:27.270 --> 00:22:32.130
allowed us to feel much more
comfortable about giving out

00:22:32.130 --> 00:22:35.670
the keys to projects, because
we could still put some controls

00:22:35.670 --> 00:22:37.590
in place, right?

00:22:37.590 --> 00:22:40.290
We could put a policy on
saying no one can create

00:22:40.290 --> 00:22:41.970
a resource with
public IP addresses,

00:22:41.970 --> 00:22:44.520
because, I don't know
about at your company,

00:22:44.520 --> 00:22:46.200
but our security
folks aren't very

00:22:46.200 --> 00:22:48.630
keen on workloads that
should be internal

00:22:48.630 --> 00:22:52.560
having public IP addresses.

00:22:52.560 --> 00:22:58.140
We were also able to integrate
our organizational Stackdriver

00:22:58.140 --> 00:23:00.780
logs into Splunk.

00:23:00.780 --> 00:23:03.610
We also ran multiple
work streams in parallel.

00:23:03.610 --> 00:23:05.970
So we had several
conference rooms

00:23:05.970 --> 00:23:08.670
that were dedicated to
the sprint engagement.

00:23:08.670 --> 00:23:10.680
And we had sub-teams
that were breaking off.

00:23:10.680 --> 00:23:15.030
It was a lot of planned
chaos for the week

00:23:15.030 --> 00:23:17.790
that they were on-site
with all the things

00:23:17.790 --> 00:23:20.010
that we had going on,
but it was great to be

00:23:20.010 --> 00:23:22.620
able to make a lot of progress
in a short period of time.

00:23:28.020 --> 00:23:31.460
That kind of gets us into
one of the use cases.

00:23:31.460 --> 00:23:35.210
So we had four
application teams that we

00:23:35.210 --> 00:23:39.090
were able to bring up
on GCP during the week

00:23:39.090 --> 00:23:44.430
of our engagement that had
never used a Google Cloud

00:23:44.430 --> 00:23:46.770
Platform before.

00:23:46.770 --> 00:23:50.070
And like Ed was saying,
we wanted self-service.

00:23:50.070 --> 00:23:51.120
We want automation.

00:23:51.120 --> 00:23:56.260
We want application teams to be
able to do things themselves.

00:23:56.260 --> 00:23:59.980
So those application
teams, they had support.

00:23:59.980 --> 00:24:01.110
We were in the room.

00:24:01.110 --> 00:24:02.590
We had Google in the room.

00:24:02.590 --> 00:24:04.890
But our attempt was--

00:24:04.890 --> 00:24:06.630
which we were successful at--

00:24:06.630 --> 00:24:08.970
was giving the application
teams the tools

00:24:08.970 --> 00:24:11.910
they needed to deploy
their own resources,

00:24:11.910 --> 00:24:14.220
not to have us to
deploy the resources,

00:24:14.220 --> 00:24:17.940
to have them script out
their application installs

00:24:17.940 --> 00:24:21.660
so that they'd
have repeatability.

00:24:21.660 --> 00:24:26.590
So one of those teams was our
Splunk infrastructure team.

00:24:26.590 --> 00:24:30.650
So Cardinal's been in the
cloud a few years now.

00:24:30.650 --> 00:24:33.429
Our Splunk infrastructure is
on another cloud platform.

00:24:33.429 --> 00:24:34.970
For many different
reasons, we wanted

00:24:34.970 --> 00:24:36.800
to move that into Google.

00:24:36.800 --> 00:24:40.640
So during the spring engagement,
we moved our dev environment

00:24:40.640 --> 00:24:46.360
from another cloud provider
that likes the color orange

00:24:46.360 --> 00:24:48.215
into GCP.

00:24:52.490 --> 00:24:55.640
At the end of the sprint, they
had built deployment manager

00:24:55.640 --> 00:24:58.100
scripts for their whole stack.

00:24:58.100 --> 00:25:04.407
They had migrated subsets
of their data over into GCP

00:25:04.407 --> 00:25:05.990
so that they knew
what they were doing

00:25:05.990 --> 00:25:07.156
when they got to production.

00:25:07.156 --> 00:25:11.160
They were moving
subsets of sample data.

00:25:11.160 --> 00:25:14.600
And really prepared themselves
for a successful production

00:25:14.600 --> 00:25:16.730
migration the following month.

00:25:16.730 --> 00:25:18.890
So Splunk was the first
production application

00:25:18.890 --> 00:25:25.370
we had running in GCP about
a month after our engagement.

00:25:25.370 --> 00:25:27.350
It also laid the
foundation for them

00:25:27.350 --> 00:25:30.260
to be able to explore
an elastic environment.

00:25:30.260 --> 00:25:33.680
So in our prior
cloud provider, there

00:25:33.680 --> 00:25:36.100
wasn't this concept of projects.

00:25:36.100 --> 00:25:38.210
There's accounts,
but you can't easily

00:25:38.210 --> 00:25:42.020
manage hundreds or
thousands of accounts.

00:25:42.020 --> 00:25:43.840
So it was a multi-tenant
environment.

00:25:43.840 --> 00:25:47.240
People didn't have the ability
to spin up their own resources,

00:25:47.240 --> 00:25:49.130
because if you gave
them the ability

00:25:49.130 --> 00:25:51.260
to spin up and delete
their resources,

00:25:51.260 --> 00:25:54.440
they could spin up and delete
other people's resources, too.

00:25:54.440 --> 00:25:56.910
Not a good thing.

00:25:56.910 --> 00:26:01.640
So GCP allowed them to build
an elastic infrastructure.

00:26:01.640 --> 00:26:04.880
So as they need
to add resources,

00:26:04.880 --> 00:26:05.970
they can add resources.

00:26:05.970 --> 00:26:09.830
They don't need to engage a
the cloud team to add resources

00:26:09.830 --> 00:26:10.960
to their environment.

00:26:10.960 --> 00:26:14.360
They modify a script
and execute it,

00:26:14.360 --> 00:26:18.860
and they have more indexers
or more search heads.

00:26:23.360 --> 00:26:26.090
Like I said, since we could
delegate more permissions,

00:26:26.090 --> 00:26:29.060
they're able to explore and
improve upon their processes

00:26:29.060 --> 00:26:32.480
rather than be dependent
on a centralized team

00:26:32.480 --> 00:26:34.920
to do everything for them.

00:26:34.920 --> 00:26:40.640
So that was our experience with
the Google Sprint Engagement.

00:26:40.640 --> 00:26:45.860
And really, like I said before,
Google met us where we were at.

00:26:45.860 --> 00:26:51.800
So if you feel like you're
not sure where to go,

00:26:51.800 --> 00:26:53.630
they can get you to
where you need to go.

00:26:53.630 --> 00:26:56.880
If you feel like
you've got a good plan,

00:26:56.880 --> 00:26:59.370
but you want a
little bit of backup,

00:26:59.370 --> 00:27:02.330
make sure that someone
that's done this before

00:27:02.330 --> 00:27:04.610
is making sure that you're
not doing something stupid,

00:27:04.610 --> 00:27:07.350
it was great for us.

00:27:07.350 --> 00:27:12.260
It really was effective for
Google to be able to meet us

00:27:12.260 --> 00:27:15.160
where we were at
in our lifecycle.

00:27:15.160 --> 00:27:19.730
I've engaged many vendors
in my years in IT,

00:27:19.730 --> 00:27:23.660
and I do have to say that the
Google Professional Services

00:27:23.660 --> 00:27:27.830
folks are far and away
more experienced and more

00:27:27.830 --> 00:27:30.170
knowledgeable than
any other vendor

00:27:30.170 --> 00:27:33.890
that I've brought
professional services in for.

00:27:33.890 --> 00:27:40.670
So it was definitely an
awesome experience for us.

00:27:40.670 --> 00:27:43.190
And with that, I'm going
to turn some time over

00:27:43.190 --> 00:27:46.910
to Walker Miller, who's going
to talk to you about our efforts

00:27:46.910 --> 00:27:49.739
with containers
during the sprint.

00:27:49.739 --> 00:27:50.655
WALKER MILLER: Thanks.

00:27:55.074 --> 00:27:56.547
See if this worked.

00:28:03.930 --> 00:28:05.012
All right.

00:28:05.012 --> 00:28:08.360
So probably like a lot
of enterprises out there,

00:28:08.360 --> 00:28:12.020
we got a go forward
strategy of utilizing kind

00:28:12.020 --> 00:28:15.760
of for in-house development,
a combination of PaaS and FaaS

00:28:15.760 --> 00:28:18.950
was kind of our first approach.

00:28:18.950 --> 00:28:22.820
But when you look at
Cardinal's compute footprint,

00:28:22.820 --> 00:28:25.490
we're really only about 15%.

00:28:25.490 --> 00:28:29.990
Internally, the developed
applications, we have about 85%

00:28:29.990 --> 00:28:32.680
common, off-the-shelf software.

00:28:32.680 --> 00:28:34.760
And obviously, we can't
do a lot with that

00:28:34.760 --> 00:28:38.000
as far as trying to
determine what we think

00:28:38.000 --> 00:28:39.530
might be a best practice for it.

00:28:39.530 --> 00:28:41.750
We have a limitation.

00:28:41.750 --> 00:28:44.300
What we've kind of
identified is probably

00:28:44.300 --> 00:28:48.500
the biggest possibility for
all that stuff is to move them

00:28:48.500 --> 00:28:49.760
into containers in Kubernetes.

00:28:49.760 --> 00:28:52.580
We see a lot of
cost optimization

00:28:52.580 --> 00:28:54.530
and just a lot of
benefits that you

00:28:54.530 --> 00:28:56.540
get from living in
kind of a container

00:28:56.540 --> 00:29:00.990
world with consistency,
speed, et cetera, right?

00:29:00.990 --> 00:29:05.300
So as part of that, we have
very limited experience

00:29:05.300 --> 00:29:07.550
when it comes to
containers and Kubernetes.

00:29:07.550 --> 00:29:09.380
We've had some success
in the PaaS world,

00:29:09.380 --> 00:29:12.810
but that's largely because they
build the containers for you.

00:29:12.810 --> 00:29:15.091
And we're going to have a
team to really support or do

00:29:15.091 --> 00:29:15.590
any of that.

00:29:15.590 --> 00:29:18.700
So we get to kind of
start from scratch.

00:29:18.700 --> 00:29:20.450
And so it was pretty
easy for us to choose

00:29:20.450 --> 00:29:23.030
to kind of partner
with Google very early

00:29:23.030 --> 00:29:26.480
on to help us kind
of build that muscle.

00:29:26.480 --> 00:29:29.360
And so, we brought them
in for that first sprint,

00:29:29.360 --> 00:29:30.860
which was about a week long.

00:29:30.860 --> 00:29:33.530
We had a track
that was two days.

00:29:33.530 --> 00:29:37.820
And we just said, let's get
a container done, right?

00:29:37.820 --> 00:29:39.740
And that's what we
started with was just one.

00:29:39.740 --> 00:29:41.990
So we took kind of
what we identified

00:29:41.990 --> 00:29:46.040
as some of our
container-friendly partners.

00:29:46.040 --> 00:29:49.750
In our case, we focused in on
IBM and their WebSphere stack,

00:29:49.750 --> 00:29:52.730
WebSPhere Application Server,
WebSphere Message Broker,

00:29:52.730 --> 00:29:55.606
and WebSphere MQ
are all things that

00:29:55.606 --> 00:29:57.230
work pretty well in
the container world

00:29:57.230 --> 00:29:59.750
and IBM has a good
level of support for.

00:29:59.750 --> 00:30:02.690
So we weren't just kind
of out on a limb on it.

00:30:02.690 --> 00:30:05.510
Nobody's too excited about using
those technologies anymore,

00:30:05.510 --> 00:30:07.220
but they still exist for us.

00:30:07.220 --> 00:30:09.710
And they're not going to
go away, unfortunately,

00:30:09.710 --> 00:30:11.640
anytime soon.

00:30:11.640 --> 00:30:13.770
So we took the first
steps, and within two days,

00:30:13.770 --> 00:30:16.950
we were able to get one of
each of those up and running.

00:30:16.950 --> 00:30:20.450
And again, we had one person
from each of those teams

00:30:20.450 --> 00:30:22.610
internally working with Google.

00:30:22.610 --> 00:30:23.720
And neither of them had--

00:30:23.720 --> 00:30:26.660
none of them had done anything
with containers before.

00:30:26.660 --> 00:30:29.360
So that sprint ended up
being as much as we could

00:30:29.360 --> 00:30:31.130
get done in two days, we did.

00:30:31.130 --> 00:30:34.556
We had a good idea for what
the process would look like.

00:30:34.556 --> 00:30:36.680
However, you're only going
to make so much progress

00:30:36.680 --> 00:30:37.320
in two days.

00:30:37.320 --> 00:30:40.850
So we brought Google back
for an additional month

00:30:40.850 --> 00:30:43.861
to focus in on just the
WebSphere application server

00:30:43.861 --> 00:30:44.360
stack.

00:30:44.360 --> 00:30:46.340
And our goal with
that was to get five

00:30:46.340 --> 00:30:48.410
to eight applications done.

00:30:48.410 --> 00:30:49.557
And we did.

00:30:49.557 --> 00:30:51.140
And really, what we
wanted to focus on

00:30:51.140 --> 00:30:52.940
was building a factory.

00:30:52.940 --> 00:30:56.539
So WebSphere applications
are pretty cut and paste

00:30:56.539 --> 00:30:58.580
for the most part once
you get it up and running.

00:30:58.580 --> 00:31:01.040
And we wanted that
full-blown kind of pipeline

00:31:01.040 --> 00:31:03.750
that we could use to
build our containers.

00:31:03.750 --> 00:31:06.590
And so, Google came
in, helped us out.

00:31:06.590 --> 00:31:08.960
And we were able to-- by
the end of that month long,

00:31:08.960 --> 00:31:10.580
we had a factory.

00:31:10.580 --> 00:31:15.890
We had five applications up
and running, at least one

00:31:15.890 --> 00:31:17.780
of them fully tested.

00:31:17.780 --> 00:31:19.934
And we, more importantly,
had a pipeline.

00:31:19.934 --> 00:31:21.350
So since they've
been there, we've

00:31:21.350 --> 00:31:23.540
actually gotten an
additional six applications

00:31:23.540 --> 00:31:25.070
that are up and
running, including

00:31:25.070 --> 00:31:27.590
in our e-commerce
application, which

00:31:27.590 --> 00:31:30.580
is our big bread
and butter, right?

00:31:30.580 --> 00:31:33.710
And that's a full-blown
WebSphere stack.

00:31:33.710 --> 00:31:35.030
I won't go into detail.

00:31:35.030 --> 00:31:39.485
So it was very, very,
very successful for us.

00:31:39.485 --> 00:31:40.940
Yeah.

00:31:40.940 --> 00:31:41.470
That's it.

00:31:41.470 --> 00:31:43.140
I'm good.

00:31:43.140 --> 00:31:43.640
Back to you.

00:31:47.140 --> 00:31:48.910
GABRIELE GARZOGLIO:
Thank you, Walker.

00:31:48.910 --> 00:31:51.160
All right, very good.

00:31:51.160 --> 00:31:53.560
So I wanted to
spend the remaining

00:31:53.560 --> 00:31:56.950
time I have to talk a bit
about the type of decisions

00:31:56.950 --> 00:32:00.700
you need to take when
you go on implementing

00:32:00.700 --> 00:32:02.320
your automation for
the provisioning

00:32:02.320 --> 00:32:05.900
of the foundational elements.

00:32:05.900 --> 00:32:09.610
Ed has talked a bit about
the use cases on networking,

00:32:09.610 --> 00:32:13.360
and Louis has mentioned
some of these elements.

00:32:13.360 --> 00:32:16.780
I wanted to spend a bit
more time on these four

00:32:16.780 --> 00:32:20.530
on the board, the bold ones.

00:32:20.530 --> 00:32:22.420
So selection of the first mover.

00:32:22.420 --> 00:32:25.010
I mentioned that it's important.

00:32:25.010 --> 00:32:27.070
There are some business
characteristics

00:32:27.070 --> 00:32:29.200
and some technical
characteristics

00:32:29.200 --> 00:32:31.360
of a good first mover.

00:32:31.360 --> 00:32:33.419
If we talk about the
business characteristics,

00:32:33.419 --> 00:32:34.960
you want something
that is not really

00:32:34.960 --> 00:32:37.300
business critical for
your first example.

00:32:37.300 --> 00:32:40.420
But on the other hand, it
shouldn't be an edge case.

00:32:40.420 --> 00:32:44.170
You want to use this first
mover as the way you go back

00:32:44.170 --> 00:32:48.490
to your executive, saying,
look, we have migrated X,

00:32:48.490 --> 00:32:50.110
and this is important to us.

00:32:50.110 --> 00:32:52.720
Let's make sure that now
that we have migrated X

00:32:52.720 --> 00:32:54.220
that we can migrate
everything else,

00:32:54.220 --> 00:32:59.230
and you get support because
of the importance of x.

00:32:59.230 --> 00:33:02.530
You can actually then build
the knowledge base out

00:33:02.530 --> 00:33:04.190
of this experience.

00:33:04.190 --> 00:33:06.916
And then, of course, we can
afford the cutover window,

00:33:06.916 --> 00:33:07.790
et cetera, et cetera.

00:33:07.790 --> 00:33:13.060
It doesn't have compliance
challenges, ideally.

00:33:13.060 --> 00:33:15.430
On the technical side,
you want something

00:33:15.430 --> 00:33:20.350
that is really not very
dependent on other services.

00:33:20.350 --> 00:33:24.070
As you start moving the
application to the cloud,

00:33:24.070 --> 00:33:26.260
you don't want to have to
take everything else that

00:33:26.260 --> 00:33:27.850
comes with it.

00:33:27.850 --> 00:33:31.540
You know, maybe it has less
integration with GCP services,

00:33:31.540 --> 00:33:33.230
and so on, and so forth.

00:33:33.230 --> 00:33:36.310
I mean, pictorially, if you
look at a design diagram,

00:33:36.310 --> 00:33:42.210
like maybe that box, the red
box, may be a good first mover.

00:33:42.210 --> 00:33:45.770
Doesn't have a lot of
dependencies with other things.

00:33:45.770 --> 00:33:48.540
So maybe it can be deployed
with CI/CD techniques.

00:33:48.540 --> 00:33:51.700
And maybe this one is not
so good because of the way

00:33:51.700 --> 00:33:53.880
it depends on everything else.

00:33:53.880 --> 00:33:57.700
What we do, we go through
diagrams like this with you,

00:33:57.700 --> 00:34:02.090
and we help you select
what a good first mover is.

00:34:02.090 --> 00:34:04.880
Then resource organization
and access management.

00:34:04.880 --> 00:34:09.070
Now, Louis was mentioning before
that they were implementing

00:34:09.070 --> 00:34:13.449
security policies, for
example, to prevent

00:34:13.449 --> 00:34:15.969
the provisioning of public IPs.

00:34:15.969 --> 00:34:19.929
Now, this is what
you want to think

00:34:19.929 --> 00:34:23.560
about when you want to then
implement security and access

00:34:23.560 --> 00:34:25.300
policies.

00:34:25.300 --> 00:34:28.030
The resource hierarchy
allows you to do

00:34:28.030 --> 00:34:29.889
good separation of duties.

00:34:29.889 --> 00:34:33.370
It allows you to implement
the access security policies.

00:34:33.370 --> 00:34:36.280
Building isolation is
at the project levels.

00:34:36.280 --> 00:34:39.469
And then you can
control quota and limits

00:34:39.469 --> 00:34:41.610
through this hierarchy.

00:34:41.610 --> 00:34:44.330
And generally, what
we recommend is, well,

00:34:44.330 --> 00:34:47.469
let's start thinking about
the simplest infrastructure

00:34:47.469 --> 00:34:49.480
we can design for you.

00:34:49.480 --> 00:34:51.280
And this is actually
a very simple one,

00:34:51.280 --> 00:34:53.030
where you have projects.

00:34:53.030 --> 00:34:56.320
And then you put them
together into folders

00:34:56.320 --> 00:34:58.270
that are environment-specific.

00:34:58.270 --> 00:35:01.180
Now, each one of these
nodes in this hierarchy

00:35:01.180 --> 00:35:05.080
is a place where you
can actually implement

00:35:05.080 --> 00:35:06.520
access and security policies.

00:35:06.520 --> 00:35:09.690
So you can say, well,
in the dev environment,

00:35:09.690 --> 00:35:11.740
I'm not going to
have my developers

00:35:11.740 --> 00:35:13.960
provision public IPs,
but maybe on production,

00:35:13.960 --> 00:35:15.280
I do need public IPs.

00:35:15.280 --> 00:35:18.430
And so, you can change
the policies that way.

00:35:18.430 --> 00:35:19.660
Now, this is simple.

00:35:19.660 --> 00:35:22.450
But in this example, for
example, where do you

00:35:22.450 --> 00:35:24.530
place your shared services?

00:35:24.530 --> 00:35:27.310
And so, then maybe you
can think about something

00:35:27.310 --> 00:35:30.270
that is a bit more complex,
where you maybe still retain

00:35:30.270 --> 00:35:32.620
the environment-oriented
approach.

00:35:32.620 --> 00:35:34.300
But then you maybe
have something that

00:35:34.300 --> 00:35:38.310
is shared-services oriented.

00:35:38.310 --> 00:35:40.390
And, you know, this is good.

00:35:40.390 --> 00:35:44.460
It's enable the separation
for shared services.

00:35:44.460 --> 00:35:48.160
It preserves their
environment separation.

00:35:48.160 --> 00:35:51.520
But the drawback is that
it's more complex than before

00:35:51.520 --> 00:35:55.660
and maybe you need still
something more complex because,

00:35:55.660 --> 00:35:58.030
for example, if you have
multiple business units,

00:35:58.030 --> 00:36:00.140
where are you going to put them?

00:36:00.140 --> 00:36:01.780
And so, we can go
on in the discussion

00:36:01.780 --> 00:36:03.520
and maybe you can
add another layer.

00:36:03.520 --> 00:36:05.920
You can have at the
top the business units.

00:36:05.920 --> 00:36:08.380
And you end up
having something that

00:36:08.380 --> 00:36:11.470
is pretty granular for
your access controls.

00:36:11.470 --> 00:36:12.910
You know, it's
easily extensible.

00:36:12.910 --> 00:36:16.060
You have a new business
unit that you want on board,

00:36:16.060 --> 00:36:17.672
it's easy to do.

00:36:17.672 --> 00:36:19.130
You know, it's a
complex structure,

00:36:19.130 --> 00:36:20.870
so it comes with complexity.

00:36:20.870 --> 00:36:23.500
The fact that you have to
manage your groups or your roles

00:36:23.500 --> 00:36:24.800
very carefully.

00:36:24.800 --> 00:36:26.380
And also, the network topology.

00:36:26.380 --> 00:36:30.470
If you have, say, one project
that manages your network,

00:36:30.470 --> 00:36:33.400
then you want to be careful
about how you give permissions

00:36:33.400 --> 00:36:35.770
to other folders
in the structure

00:36:35.770 --> 00:36:38.080
and the projects
inside those folders

00:36:38.080 --> 00:36:40.260
to get access to the network.

00:36:40.260 --> 00:36:44.159
So together, we generally
all go through something

00:36:44.159 --> 00:36:45.700
like this, some
discussion like this,

00:36:45.700 --> 00:36:51.260
and then decide which one is
the best hierarchy for you.

00:36:51.260 --> 00:36:53.300
Together with the
hierarchies I mentioned

00:36:53.300 --> 00:36:55.230
goes access management.

00:36:55.230 --> 00:36:59.310
So how is the access management
implemented in Google?

00:36:59.310 --> 00:37:01.350
If you look at
this diagram here,

00:37:01.350 --> 00:37:03.860
you have that you have
identities at the top.

00:37:03.860 --> 00:37:07.400
You have individual
identities, robo identities,

00:37:07.400 --> 00:37:09.700
or service accounts, and
then you have groups.

00:37:09.700 --> 00:37:12.730
Groups can be a
collection of those two.

00:37:12.730 --> 00:37:16.380
These identities
are granted a role.

00:37:16.380 --> 00:37:21.910
And the role is a set of
permissions over resources.

00:37:21.910 --> 00:37:24.910
What we recommend
always is that you

00:37:24.910 --> 00:37:29.120
give access to your
resources, role

00:37:29.120 --> 00:37:32.530
to your resources from a
group, because, this way, it's

00:37:32.530 --> 00:37:36.040
easier for you to
manage the membership.

00:37:36.040 --> 00:37:38.650
If people come and go,
you have a structure

00:37:38.650 --> 00:37:40.130
that is well thought out of.

00:37:40.130 --> 00:37:43.240
And to the zeroth
level, what we recommend

00:37:43.240 --> 00:37:46.660
is to start simply with maybe
for organizational level

00:37:46.660 --> 00:37:47.810
groups.

00:37:47.810 --> 00:37:52.000
We have the org admin, you
know, defines the IAM policies.

00:37:52.000 --> 00:37:56.980
It can implement the structure
that we have discussed before,

00:37:56.980 --> 00:37:58.650
can create projects.

00:37:58.650 --> 00:38:00.910
Although, eventually, maybe
this responsibility can be

00:38:00.910 --> 00:38:02.410
delegated.

00:38:02.410 --> 00:38:04.990
You have a network
administrator group

00:38:04.990 --> 00:38:07.910
that has the responsibility for
creating the network topology

00:38:07.910 --> 00:38:10.780
that then the rest of your
environment is going to use.

00:38:14.680 --> 00:38:18.140
The network administrators
in this picture

00:38:18.140 --> 00:38:22.220
maintain the firewall
rules, and you

00:38:22.220 --> 00:38:24.620
can decide whether this
function is something

00:38:24.620 --> 00:38:26.900
that the network administrators
do or the security

00:38:26.900 --> 00:38:28.130
administrators do.

00:38:28.130 --> 00:38:30.920
That's part of the discussions
that we're going to have.

00:38:30.920 --> 00:38:33.370
You have a security
admin that, at a minimum,

00:38:33.370 --> 00:38:39.200
has visibility on the resources,
establishes the role--

00:38:39.200 --> 00:38:43.270
so the custom roles that Louis
was talking about before--

00:38:43.270 --> 00:38:44.940
and then establishes
the policies

00:38:44.940 --> 00:38:47.780
at the organizational
nodes of the structure

00:38:47.780 --> 00:38:49.460
that we were discussing before.

00:38:49.460 --> 00:38:53.370
And then you have somebody that
is responsible for setting up

00:38:53.370 --> 00:38:56.450
the building accounts,
monitoring the usage,

00:38:56.450 --> 00:38:59.630
granting access to using
the billing accounts

00:38:59.630 --> 00:39:02.280
to project owners, and so forth.

00:39:02.280 --> 00:39:04.940
So, you know, these are what
we recommended as a minimum

00:39:04.940 --> 00:39:06.597
for organizational level.

00:39:06.597 --> 00:39:08.180
And then, of course,
there are folder-

00:39:08.180 --> 00:39:09.740
and project-level groups.

00:39:09.740 --> 00:39:14.150
And these actually change a
lot depending on your business.

00:39:14.150 --> 00:39:18.320
You always have some kind of
reliability engineer or some

00:39:18.320 --> 00:39:22.805
kind of DevOps operation ,
where the developers develop

00:39:22.805 --> 00:39:25.820
in an environment and
then collaborate with

00:39:25.820 --> 00:39:29.030
the reliability engineers to
actually do the deployment

00:39:29.030 --> 00:39:30.290
through CI/CD.

00:39:30.290 --> 00:39:35.900
And then you might have some
kind of data analyst figure

00:39:35.900 --> 00:39:39.680
that you want to
create a group for.

00:39:39.680 --> 00:39:42.260
Now, for every one
of these groups,

00:39:42.260 --> 00:39:44.810
we recommend a set of roles.

00:39:44.810 --> 00:39:49.480
And as I said, these rules
are sets of permissions

00:39:49.480 --> 00:39:50.940
under the resources.

00:39:50.940 --> 00:39:52.020
So in this example--

00:39:52.020 --> 00:39:53.720
I have only one example--

00:39:53.720 --> 00:39:57.230
you have the compute
network admin that creates,

00:39:57.230 --> 00:40:00.170
modify, and delete
networking resources,

00:40:00.170 --> 00:40:03.320
except for firewall rules
and SSL certificates.

00:40:03.320 --> 00:40:06.650
As I said before, you can decide
whether to give that permission

00:40:06.650 --> 00:40:10.507
to the network administrators
or your security administrators,

00:40:10.507 --> 00:40:11.340
and so on, so forth.

00:40:14.680 --> 00:40:18.640
Now, one thing that we also do,
start talking about operations.

00:40:18.640 --> 00:40:21.640
Once you have the
foundational layout,

00:40:21.640 --> 00:40:26.590
you have your application
in your environment,

00:40:26.590 --> 00:40:28.630
you need to make sense of it.

00:40:28.630 --> 00:40:31.660
And operations has
multiple Facets.

00:40:31.660 --> 00:40:33.760
One thing that I thought
I'd talk about with you

00:40:33.760 --> 00:40:37.360
is really that
billing and analytics.

00:40:37.360 --> 00:40:39.160
One thing that we
always recommend

00:40:39.160 --> 00:40:43.990
is make sure that when you
create your organization,

00:40:43.990 --> 00:40:46.660
you go to your account,
billing account,

00:40:46.660 --> 00:40:50.170
and you export the
logs to be queried.

00:40:50.170 --> 00:40:52.340
This allows you to do
at least two things.

00:40:52.340 --> 00:40:57.430
One thing is you can use SQL
syntax to query your billing

00:40:57.430 --> 00:40:58.340
information.

00:40:58.340 --> 00:40:59.920
And so you can
start having insight

00:40:59.920 --> 00:41:02.220
in your billing information.

00:41:02.220 --> 00:41:04.270
The other thing you can
deploy out of the box

00:41:04.270 --> 00:41:10.810
is Data Studio dashboard
that can display information

00:41:10.810 --> 00:41:16.810
about your billing so that, as
you configure these dashboards,

00:41:16.810 --> 00:41:19.660
you can create
automatically reports

00:41:19.660 --> 00:41:25.420
that maybe you can share with
your CFO or with your VPs.

00:41:25.420 --> 00:41:28.250
Key to doing good
billing is really

00:41:28.250 --> 00:41:32.710
defining the labels that
characterize your resources.

00:41:32.710 --> 00:41:36.970
So labels can be, for example,
associated to cost centers,

00:41:36.970 --> 00:41:39.820
maybe to business units.

00:41:39.820 --> 00:41:41.934
When we, PSO, come
on-site with you,

00:41:41.934 --> 00:41:43.600
we have that discussion
with you, right?

00:41:43.600 --> 00:41:48.400
How should we organize the
labels to view your billing?

00:41:48.400 --> 00:41:50.740
At a minimum, you
have the granularity

00:41:50.740 --> 00:41:52.880
at the project level.

